{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>You shall surely return to him the pledge.</b> Even if it remained with him many years, he is obligated to return it — he always returns it and takes it back and returns it again, until a court finalizes it for him. And this is 'השב תשיב לו את העבוט' — as long as it is his pledge; and likewise they said: 'לא תשכב בעבוטו' — you shall not sleep, and his pledge is with you. Now, 'his pledge' — this is a daytime garment or a nighttime garment or vessels of a living soul's food; but other vessels, he is not obligated to return, even though he needs them, and even at the time when he needs them." "<b>And you should</b> seek to understand here the secret of the commandment in what it says: 'כבא השמש' — that is, if you do not return it and the night passes — the time of the dominion of the attribute of judgment — it will encounter you. <b>And he shall sleep in his garment, and bless you.</b> The attribute of mercy. <b>And it shall be righteousness for you.</b> That is, merit before YHWH your Elohim." "<b>On his day you shall give him his wage.</b> For his benefit. <b>And the sun shall not set upon him.</b> For your benefit. 'ביומו תתן שכרו' is joined with 'ולא יקרא,' and the reading of the verse is thus: 'On his day you shall give him his wage, for he is poor' — and one who withholds his hire from him is like one who withholds his very life, and since he withholds his life, he is dead. From here they said that the poor man is accounted as dead; this is what it says: 'כי עני הוא ואליו הוא נושא את נפשו.' 'And the sun shall not set upon him,' so that he does not cry out against you to HaShem — for if the sun sets upon him and he cries out, there will be sin in you at night, when the attribute of judgment holds sway and stretches out against you."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {2 "The phrase 'ולא יקרא' appears to reference Deuteronomy 24:15 ('ולא יקרא עליך אל ה'), which R. Bahya quotes further on; 'ולא תבא עליו השמש' follows the text's reading of that verse, where the ketiv has בוא (set/come upon) read as though from נבא or simply taken literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:57.772845062Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}